---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';

const site = siteFromHost();
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-brevo';
const title = 'Drip vs Brevo for Shopify Email: 2026 Buyer Guide';
const desc = 'Compare Drip lifecycle workflows with Brevo PushOwl Shopify sync, consent migration, CRM and transactional messaging responsibilities, and matched cost forecasts.';
const faqs = [
  { question: 'Is Drip or Brevo better for a Shopify-only store?', answer: 'Start with the work you need to operate. Drip documents Shopify customer, order and product sync and ecommerce workflow triggers. Brevo PushOwl also supports Shopify recovery and welcome journeys. Rehearse a product education flow and a purchase exit in each; do not assume a general suite lacks ecommerce tools or that a specialist is automatically easier.' },
  { question: 'Which is cheaper: Drip or Brevo?', answer: 'Request quotes for the same imported population, campaign calendar, automation traffic and required modules. Include peak sends, implementation and any sender you retain. Confirm the billing treatment of imported and unsubscribed profiles rather than pricing only the list you intend to email. This guide does not quote supplier prices.' },
  { question: 'Can Brevo replace Drip for Shopify lifecycle email?', answer: 'It is a candidate, using the supported Brevo PushOwl connector. Replacement depends on the actual events, product fields, purchase exits and consent records your current journeys use. Importing into an existing Brevo account may overwrite subscription status; review that separately from the ongoing consent source-of-truth setting before enabling marketing.' },
  { question: 'What should a 30-day pilot prove?', answer: 'Treat 30 days as a proposed planning window, not a migration promise. Prove initial import and ongoing consent updates separately, then product education, purchase suppression and notification ownership. A second operator should explain an unexpected enrollment from the event history. Keep production sending paused until the relevant checks pass.' },
  { question: 'Which alternatives deserve a pilot?', answer: 'Add another candidate only for a named gap that neither setup solves, such as a required event or an existing sales workflow. Keeping Shopify notifications and a separate CRM may be a workable option alongside Drip. An existing Brevo team should compare the effort of connecting PushOwl with the effort of rebuilding its marketing elsewhere.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a><span class="mx-2 text-[#5d3a1d]">/</span><span class="text-[#5d3a1d]">Drip vs Brevo</span></nav>
      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="editorial-kicker">Head-to-head buyer guide</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Drip vs Brevo for Shopify email</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Should Shopify retention live in a dedicated lifecycle workspace, or join the Brevo account that already handles other customer messaging? Compare the work of maintaining product journeys with the work of connecting accounts, reconciling consent and assigning each sender a clear responsibility.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl"><TldrBox picks={[
        { label: 'Lifecycle candidate', tool: 'Drip', reason: 'Evaluate Shopify purchase and product context in the journeys your operator maintains.' },
        { label: 'Suite candidate', tool: 'Brevo', reason: 'Evaluate PushOwl alongside the CRM or transactional work you actually need.' },
      ]} /></div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>What each setup brings to the decision</h2>
        <p><a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424695491725-Connect-Your-Shopify-Store">Drip’s Shopify guide</a> describes customer, order and product sync, purchase-history segmentation and ecommerce triggers. Its app embed provides page and product-view tracking. These are useful ingredients for a retention owner building category education and repeat-purchase branches. The trade-off to investigate is the effort of maintaining those journeys alongside any separate sales or service tools.</p>
        <p><a href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360014912879-Brevo-PushOwl-app-for-Shopify-Connect-your-Shopify-store-with-Brevo-PushOwl">Brevo identifies PushOwl as its supported Shopify connector</a>, replacing the former app for new stores. Its documented uses include recovery and welcome journeys. Brevo should therefore receive a real ecommerce rehearsal, rather than being dismissed as a list-only integration. For an existing Brevo team, the potential benefit is retaining its customer messaging work while adding Shopify data; the cost is reconciling account and permission behavior before sending.</p>
        <div class="card overflow-x-auto"><table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Drip rehearsal</th><th>Brevo rehearsal</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Product education</td><td>Trace purchase and product context through a branch.</td><td>Inspect the fields and events available through PushOwl.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Existing customer records</td><td>Check matched profiles and subscription state after sync.</td><td>Confirm the intended account and import conflict behavior.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sales relationship</td><td>Define the handoff to the CRM you retain.</td><td>Verify the sales module, permissions and handoff needed in your account.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Service notifications</td><td>Document which Shopify or external sender remains responsible.</td><td>Test a separate transactional route only if consolidation is required.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>

        <h2>The migration risk: initial import is not ongoing consent sync</h2>
        <p>Brevo’s setup guide warns that importing Shopify data may overwrite existing contact attributes, including subscription status. Before importing into an established account, preserve a dated export of its suppression and consent records and reconcile conflicting profiles. Connecting the correct account matters: a newly created account will not automatically contain the sales and campaign history your team expects.</p>
        <p>PushOwl’s <a href="https://docs.pushowl.com/en/articles/14660088-source-of-truth-for-marketing-consent">consent source-of-truth guide</a> distinguishes ongoing updates from the historical import. The setting governs real-time marketing consent, not product or order sync; it is not a blanket safeguard for the first import. Directional overrides can also affect which consent state is preserved.</p>
        <p>Proposed conflict test: use a controlled profile that is subscribed in Shopify but suppressed in the existing Brevo account. Record both states before import, inspect the result with sending disabled, and resolve it against your actual consent evidence. Then test a subsequent update separately under the chosen settings. Include a newly created profile and an unsubscribe originating in Brevo. Keep the before/after records and the setting values so another operator can repeat the test.</p>
        <p>Drip also warrants a consent check. Its integration guide describes new-profile status based on Shopify marketing preference and updates to Shopify when a person unsubscribes or resubscribes in Drip. That documentation does not certify every conflict in your existing database. Rehearse matching, unsubscribe and any bulk-import path you plan to use; do not equate having an order history with permission to market.</p>

        <h2>Proposed lifecycle rehearsal: education followed by a repeat purchase</h2>
        <p>For a store selling refillable products, build a proposed journey that sends setup guidance after the first purchase, then offers a relevant refill when appropriate to that product. This is a test design, not an observed merchant result. Use the product identifier to distinguish a refillable starter kit from an accessory that does not need replenishment. Choose timing from your own product usage guidance rather than a universal interval.</p>
        <p>In each platform, record the incoming order and product fields, the branch taken and the planned next message. Place a repeat order before that message and inspect whether the purchase exit removes the refill promotion. Repeat with an accessory-only order and a suppressed profile. If you want delivery-based timing or subscription-aware exclusions, first establish the event source for those states; an order event alone proves neither delivery nor an active subscription.</p>
        <p>Have the next operator change the refill recommendation without changing the entry rule. Ask them to explain why a selected profile is still waiting. Drip’s ecommerce workflow model is worth evaluating for this ongoing maintenance job. Brevo’s value depends on whether the same job remains understandable alongside its other messaging responsibilities. Record actual setup and correction effort; this guide has not measured either platform’s speed or revenue effect.</p>

        <h2>CRM and transactional email are separate requirements</h2>
        <p>If sales staff follow wholesale inquiries, specify what they must record: a company, an owner, a stage and the next action. Verify the relevant Brevo sales capabilities and account entitlement with that task, rather than assuming a synced Shopify contact creates a complete sales process. For Drip, compare retaining your existing CRM and passing only the needed eligibility state into retention. Consolidation is valuable only if the sales team can still carry out its work.</p>
        <p><a href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360021196220-What-are-the-differences-between-marketing-transactional-and-automation-emails">Brevo documents SMTP and API transactional sending</a>. It also distinguishes informational messages from promotional automation content. A refill discount remains marketing even if an API or automation sends it. Keep promotional eligibility separate from service-message delivery.</p>
        <p>Do not infer that installing the Shopify connector reroutes order confirmations. Make a sender inventory for order receipts, shipping updates, password resets, recovery and product education. For any proposed change, identify the triggering system, template owner, duplicate-prevention rule and failure alert. Use controlled orders to check which sender actually fires. Keeping Shopify notifications in place may reduce migration work; moving them requires a separate implementation decision and validation.</p>

        <h2>Compare complete costs without invented price bands</h2>
        <p>Use the same imported population and sending calendar for both quotes. Drip’s integration guide warns that syncing existing Shopify customers can increase billing; ask which imported statuses count in your account. For Brevo, obtain the applicable PushOwl quote and confirm the treatment of existing Brevo services, sales users and transactional volume. A direct Brevo marketing quote alone may not describe the Shopify setup you are buying.</p>
        <p>Forecast a normal month, a promotion month and a month with list growth. Separate recurring platform charges from migration labor, connector work, retained vendors and ongoing maintenance. If you expect a saving from retiring another tool, count it only after its actual job has passed the replacement test.</p>
        <p>Hypothetical workload calculation: 8,000 eligible contacts receiving four broadcasts produce 32,000 campaign emails. Adding 6,000 lifecycle messages produces 38,000 marketing emails; 2,000 service notifications would bring total email traffic to 40,000 if consolidated. These are planning assumptions, not supplier allowances or a merchant’s results. Obtain the applicable charges for each message category and add any additional channels separately.</p>

        <h2>A decision record your team can use</h2>
        <p>Make consent preservation and purchase exits acceptance requirements. For each requirement, retain the test profile, source event, observed branch and any correction needed. Compare operator effort and complete quoted cost only after those requirements pass; an arbitrary weighted score should not average away a failed suppression rule.</p>
        <p>Choose Drip if the tested lifecycle work is maintainable and the retained CRM and notification arrangement meets your needs. Choose Brevo if the PushOwl import and ongoing sync behave as intended and the broader messaging arrangement removes work you otherwise have to maintain. If neither passes a required event or handoff, document that gap before expanding the shortlist. A proposed 30-day evaluation is a scheduling aid, not evidence that migration is safe or complete.</p>
      </article>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm"><p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p><p class="mt-2"><a class="link" href="/compare/brevo-vs-omnisend">Brevo vs Omnisend</a> · <a class="link" href="/compare/drip-vs-mailchimp">Drip vs Mailchimp</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/selection-framework">Selection framework</a></p></div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Drip vs Brevo FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
